public static boolean isPalindrome(String s) {
    if (s == null) return false;
    int left = 0;
    int right = s.length() - 1;
    while (left < right) {
        if (s.charAt(left) != s.charAt(right)) {
            return false;
        }
        left++;
        right--;
    }
    return true;
}

This compares characters from both ends moving inward. To ignore case and non-letters, first clean the string with s.toLowerCase().replaceAll("[^a-z0-9]", "").

def find_largest(numbers):
    if not numbers:
        raise ValueError("List is empty")
    largest = numbers[0]
    for n in numbers[1:]:
        if n > largest:
            largest = n
    return largest

print(find_largest([3, 9, 2, 7]))  # 9

The built-in max(numbers) does the same thing in one call.

public class PrintNumbers {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
            System.out.println(i);
        }
    }
}

This uses a for loop that starts at 1 and prints each number until it reaches 10.

def count_vowels(text):
    vowels = "aeiouAEIOU"
    return sum(1 for ch in text if ch in vowels)

print(count_vowels("Hello World"))  # 3

It loops through each character and counts those found in the vowel string, handling both uppercase and lowercase.

public static int sumArray(int[] numbers) {
    int sum = 0;
    for (int n : numbers) {
        sum += n;
    }
    return sum;
}

// Example
// sumArray(new int[]{1, 2, 3, 4}) returns 10

The method loops through the array and adds each element to a running total.

def remove_duplicates(items):
    seen = set()
    result = []
    for item in items:
        if item not in seen:
            seen.add(item)
            result.append(item)
    return result

print(remove_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 1]))  # [1, 2, 3]

This keeps the original order. If order does not matter, list(set(items)) is shorter.

def factorial(n):
    if n < 0:
        raise ValueError("n must be non-negative")
    if n == 0 or n == 1:   # base case
        return 1
    return n * factorial(n - 1)

print(factorial(5))  # 120

factorial(5) = 5 * 4 * 3 * 2 * 1. The function calls itself with a smaller number until it reaches the base case.
